  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**Event confirmed**\n\n- A late\u2011summer heat wave combined with reactor outages forced Korea Electric Power Corporation (KEPCO) into unannounced nationwide rolling blackouts on **15\u202fSeptember\u202f2011**, affecting roughly **2\u202fmillion households and industrial users**.  \n  *Evidence*: Corpus search returned a trigger record that explicitly describes the outage and its scale\u3010search_corpus\u20115\u3011.\n\n**Companies structurally at risk**\n\nThe downstream supply\u2011graph (tier\u202f\u2264\u202f2) for KEPCO identified more than one hundred firms that either purchase electricity/related services from KEPCO or depend on KEPCO\u2011linked inputs.  The most salient groups are reproduced below; the full list is available in the traversal output\u3010traverse_supply_graph\u20111\u3011.\n\n| Tier | Representative companies (sector) |\n|------|-----------------------------------|\n| **Tier\u202f1** (direct customers / immediate suppliers) | Hyundai Rotem (transport\u2011equipment), LS Electric (electrical equipment), SK (energy\u2011materials holding), POSCO (steel), Lucky Cement (construction materials), Kumho Petrochemical (synthetic rubber), Vedanta (metals & mining), Samsung C&T (engineering & construction), Hyundai Engineering\u202f&\u202fConstruction (EPC) |\n| **Tier\u202f2** (customers of Tier\u202f1 firms or alternative suppliers) | Apple (technology hardware), Hyundai Motor (automotive), LG Chem (chemicals / battery cathodes), Bridgestone (tires), Volvo AB (trucks & buses), BP (oil & gas), Samsung SDI (battery cells), Hyundai Heavy Industries (shipbuilding), Mitsubishi Heavy Industries (industrial machinery), etc. |\n\nThese firms are **structurally exposed** because they either:\n\n* purchase electricity directly from KEPCO for manufacturing/operations, or  \n* rely on components that are produced using KEPCO\u2011supplied power (e.g., steel mills, battery plants, heavy\u2011equipment factories).\n\n**Evidence gathered for specific companies**\n\n| Company | Tool(s) consulted | Findings |\n|---------|-------------------|----------|\n| **POSCO** (steel) | `get_supplier_info` (20\u2011F filed 24\u202fJun\u202f2011) | The filing predates the outage and contains no mention of KEPCO\u2019s September\u202f2011 blackout or any related operational impact. No direct evidence that POSCO was affected. |\n| **Hyundai Motor** | `search_corpus` for \u201cHyundai Motor September\u202f2011 power outage\u201d | Returned only unrelated Toyota\u2011focused excerpts; no reference to Hyundai Motor being impacted by the KEPCO event. |\n| **Apple**, **LG Chem**, **Bridgestone**, etc. (Tier\u202f2) | No company\u2011specific filings were examined (tool budget exhausted). | No evidence found in the material already retrieved. |\n\n**What could not be confirmed**\n\n- **Direct impact on any downstream firm** \u2013 The only concrete source we have is the generic event trigger describing the blackout itself. None of the company\u2011level filings or corpus mentions we accessed contain explicit statements that a listed downstream company suffered production cuts, revenue loss, supply interruptions, or other consequences from the KEPCO outage.\n- **Quantitative effect** \u2013 No data on lost output, financial hit, or mitigation actions for any specific firm could be extracted from the limited documents examined.\n- **Tier\u202f2 verification** \u2013 Because of the four\u2011call limit we were unable to retrieve filings for most Tier\u202f2 firms (Apple, Hyundai Motor, etc.). Their exposure remains inferred solely from the supply\u2011graph topology.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe September\u202f2011 KEPCO rolling blackout was a real, nationwide event that disrupted electricity service to millions. The supply\u2011graph analysis shows a broad network of downstream companies\u2014spanning steel, automotive, electronics, chemicals, construction and more\u2014that are **structurally vulnerable** to such an outage. However, within the four tool calls allowed for this investigation we could only confirm the occurrence of the blackout itself; we found **no direct documentary evidence** linking any specific downstream company (including POSCO or Hyundai Motor) to measurable adverse effects from that event.\n\nAccordingly, while the list of at\u2011risk firms is well\u2011defined by the graph, the **evidence base for actual impact remains incomplete**. Further research would require additional filings or news\u2011type disclosures for each firm to verify whether and how they were affected by KEPCO\u2019s September\u202f2011 disruption.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (106) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - hyundai rotem: no evidence gathered\n  - ls electric: no evidence gathered\n  - sk: no evidence gathered\n  - perusahaan perseroan persero pt perusahaan listrik negara: no evidence gathered\n  - lucky cement: no evidence gathered\n  - kumho petrochemical: no evidence gathered\n  - vedanta: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung c&t: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai engineering & construction: no evidence gathered\n  - apple: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai motor: no evidence gathered\n  - guangdong delian: no evidence gathered\n  - aapico hitech pcl: no evidence gathered\n  - lg chem: no evidence gathered\n  - steel pipe industry of indonesia pt: no evidence gathered\n  - bridgestone: no evidence gathered\n  - volvo ab: no evidence gathered\n  - bp: no evidence gathered\n  - saudi basic industries: no evidence gathered\n  - hd hyundai heavy industries: no evidence gathered\n  - hiroca holdings lid: no evidence gathered\n  - lg energy solution: no evidence gathered\n  - bayerische motoren werke: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung sdi: no evidence gathered\n  - medco energi internasional tbk pt: no evidence gathered\n  - hansol chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai mobis: no evidence gathered\n  - usa steel: no evidence g